<rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"><channel><language>en</language><title>Breaking news from AFRICA, Maghreb and Sub-Saharan Africa - France 24</title><description>FRANCE 24’s coverage of events, politics and social issues across Africa, including North Africa (the Maghreb) and the Sahel, aided by our network of correspondents and experts across the continent.</description><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/</link><lastBuildDate>Sat, 19 Sep 2026 11:26:27 GMT</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/rss" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><category></category><title>37 die in Nigerian custody: Reports blame overcrowding, government disputes claim</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/37-die-in-nigerian-custody-reports-blame-overcrowding-government-disputes-claim</link><description>
Authorities in north-central Nigeria are investigating the deaths of 37 people while they were in custody for illegal mining. Officials say the detainees died at a detention facility in Niger state, with the Nigerian Security and Civil Defence Corps pointing to a possible disease outbreak. However, some reports suggest overcrowding and poor ventilation may have contributed to the deaths. Details by FRANCE 24 correspondent Sam Olukoya.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/65545b5a-b41b-11f1-a160-0730212ba17f/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20260918-214253-214509-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/65545b5a-b41b-11f1-a160-0730212ba17f/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20260918-214253-214509-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">6ad88ae2-b41b-11f1-b1e4-1314eca9ae5c</guid><pubDate>Sat, 19 Sep 2026 11:26:22 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Suspected alcohol poisoning in Nigeria's Ondo State kills at least 49 people</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/suspected-alcohol-poisoning-in-nigeria-s-ondo-state-kills-at-least-49-people</link><description>
In Nigeria's Ondo state, at least 49 people have died and 100 others injured after drinking alcohol suspected to contain methanol. Local authorities say 'Monkey Shoulder', a locally brewed herbal drink with high alcohol content is believed to have been the source of the poisoning, but investigations are still ongoing. State police have announced the arrest of 15 people in connection with the incident. Story by Clemence Waller.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/5ceee4ea-b415-11f1-8462-9ff5c3f464d4/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20260919-081623-081755-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/5ceee4ea-b415-11f1-8462-9ff5c3f464d4/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20260919-081623-081755-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">62cd3416-b415-11f1-9ce2-757b5e461cca</guid><pubDate>Sat, 19 Sep 2026 11:19:32 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Ebola outbreak reaches new areas in Democratic Republic of Congo</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/ebola-outbreak-reaches-new-areas-in-democratic-republic-of-congo</link><description>
In the Democratic Republic of Congo, the government claims that the Ebola outbreak has changed course, with cases trending downwards in hotspots. But that interpretation is being met with pushback from experts and health responders, who say the outbreak remains as bad as ever. Details by FRANCE 24 correspondent Emmet Livingstone.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/869cc4c2-b40d-11f1-88bb-6f779f079bc8/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20260919-092014-092142-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/869cc4c2-b40d-11f1-88bb-6f779f079bc8/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20260919-092014-092142-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">8c62d3c4-b40d-11f1-9cd8-8bb2ea9db659</guid><pubDate>Sat, 19 Sep 2026 09:59:20 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Uproar in Niger state, 37 die in custody</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/uproar-in-niger-state-37-die-in-custody</link><description>
In tonights programme, investigation is ongoing into the death in custody of thirty-seven people, accused of illegal mining in Nigeria. Also,Tunisia is emerging as a major boat-building hub, serving both local buyers and international markets. And finally, we speak to the author of “The Moon Will Not Burn Us”, Ricci Shryock.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/e2e3a4ec-b3a1-11f1-9c84-0730212ba17f/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-7532683446aada2e9e59607-09759263.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/e2e3a4ec-b3a1-11f1-9c84-0730212ba17f/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-7532683446aada2e9e59607-09759263.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">ff096c8e-b3a0-11f1-a86b-5985cba0c557</guid><pubDate>Fri, 18 Sep 2026 20:52:51 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Hélène Daouphars</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>At least 37 suspected illegal miners die in custody in central Nigeria</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260918-37-suspected-illegal-miners-die-in-custody-in-central-nigeria</link><description>
Nigerian authorities launched an investigation Thursday after 37 suspected illegal miners died in a civil defence detention facility in central Niger state, with conflicting accounts blaming either a disease outbreak or overcrowding and poor ventilation.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/fd53c6ca-b2f4-11f1-8166-5570cf9529c5/w:1024/p:16x9/AP24346673836644.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/fd53c6ca-b2f4-11f1-8166-5570cf9529c5/w:1024/p:16x9/AP24346673836644.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">cacbb76e-b2f7-11f1-965f-853ed26dadbd</guid><pubDate>Fri, 18 Sep 2026 00:27:53 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/fd53c6ca-b2f4-11f1-8166-5570cf9529c5/w:1024/p:16x9/AP24346673836644.jpg">© Sunday Alamba, AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Serial killer suspicions in South Africa</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/serial-killer-suspicions-in-south-africa</link><description>
In tonight's edition: the body of a ninth woman is found in Johannesburg as South African police start investigating a potential femicidal serial killer. Also, Africans are excited about what good use of the technology could mean to growth, but many are also worried about what an AI free-for-all could mean for their lives and livelihoods. And Chad’s first music school officially opened its doors on Wednesday night in N’Djamena. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3611477c-b2d6-11f1-8e6b-430f3874e448/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-12071973626aac4d34225ba0-03662603.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3611477c-b2d6-11f1-8e6b-430f3874e448/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-12071973626aac4d34225ba0-03662603.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">b4367eca-b2d5-11f1-acd4-b50f014d8fc6</guid><pubDate>Thu, 17 Sep 2026 20:25:05 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Georja CALVIN-SMITH</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>DR Congo says Ebola outbreak has passed its peak</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/dr-congo-says-ebola-outbreak-has-passed-its-peak</link><description>
More than 3,510 people have died from Ebola in eastern DR Congo since the outbreak was declared four months ago, with Ituri accounting for 78% of confirmed cases. Congolese authorities say the epidemic has now passed its peak, pointing to signs that the outbreak is beginning to slow.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/251f5dca-b298-11f1-9d17-991cccd3bb73/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20260916-214314-214535-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/251f5dca-b298-11f1-9d17-991cccd3bb73/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20260916-214314-214535-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">2b0aa3a2-b298-11f1-98e1-35a318fe9dfa</guid><pubDate>Thu, 17 Sep 2026 13:36:09 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Zimbabwe farmers brace for El Niño by saving water</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20260917-zimbabwe-farmers-brace-for-el-ni%C3%B1o-by-saving-water</link><description>
Some communities in Zimbabwe are adopting water-saving techniques such as aquaponics and rainwater harvesting to protect crops as the country braces for El Niño-driven drought. Sharon Mazingaizo has this report.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1ec4d298-b298-11f1-98d9-4daf57bffe27/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20260916-215122-215323-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1ec4d298-b298-11f1-98d9-4daf57bffe27/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20260916-215122-215323-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">2540b0b0-b298-11f1-8f02-112304533c3c</guid><pubDate>Thu, 17 Sep 2026 13:33:33 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Tigray: 20,000 university students relocated by conflict</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tigray-20-000-university-students-relocated-by-conflict</link><description>
Ethiopia's Education Ministry says around 20,000 university students in Tigray will be temporarily relocated to other public campuses after accusing the TPLF of using universities as military bases. The move comes as fighting and drone strikes continue in the northern region, with many students saying the trauma of the 2020-2022 Tigray war is still fresh. Our correspondent Tom Canetti reports from Addis Ababa.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/309e90ee-b298-11f1-9648-991cccd3bb73/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20260916-214840-215041-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/309e90ee-b298-11f1-9648-991cccd3bb73/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20260916-214840-215041-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">3b0dfb50-b298-11f1-9fbe-35a318fe9dfa</guid><pubDate>Thu, 17 Sep 2026 13:30:51 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Senegal farmers turn to fish and rice farming</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/senegal-farmers-turn-to-fish-and-rice-farming</link><description>
Some farmers in northern Senegal are combining fish farming with rice cultivation, using an age-old technique that helps fertilise fields, reduce pests and diversify incomes. The climate-smart approach is gaining renewed attention as communities adapt to growing environmental pressures.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3546f17c-b298-11f1-80da-d7a11ace016d/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20260916-215341-215523-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3546f17c-b298-11f1-80da-d7a11ace016d/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20260916-215341-215523-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">3efd3a8c-b298-11f1-8b5b-df24e8f07957</guid><pubDate>Thu, 17 Sep 2026 13:28:56 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Encouraging signs in DR Congo Ebola fight</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/encouraging-signs-in-dr-congo-ebola-fight</link><description>
In tonight's edition: there are signs of a welcome slowdown in DR Congo's Ebola outbreak. Also, about 20,000 Ethiopian university students in the northern region of Tigray will be relocated to other campuses across the country. And in northern Senegal, scientists are using fish to keep their food stocks and communtites healthy.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/228a9aae-b287-11f1-98b8-07ed91e97eb2/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-7445997086aabc889270c81-54501443.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/228a9aae-b287-11f1-98b8-07ed91e97eb2/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-7445997086aabc889270c81-54501443.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">f34ccbf0-b20d-11f1-abf9-ef1aa530c3d6</guid><pubDate>Wed, 16 Sep 2026 20:42:27 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Georja CALVIN-SMITH</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>WHO sees 'encouraging signs' in DR Congo Ebola response but says outbreak 'far from over'</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260916-who-sees-encouraging-signs-in-dr-congo-ebola-response-but-says-outbreak-far-from-over</link><description>
The World Health Organization said Wednesday that the fight against the Ebola outbreak in DR Congo was showing “encouraging signs" but that the epidemic was "far from over". More than 7,000 cases have been reported across seven provinces as of September 11.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/0fff06cc-b1e2-11f1-93b2-e5727ccb1647/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-09-16T144444Z-226795097-RC2EGNAD8RRF-RTRMADP-3-HEALTH-EBOLA-WHO.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/0fff06cc-b1e2-11f1-93b2-e5727ccb1647/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-09-16T144444Z-226795097-RC2EGNAD8RRF-RTRMADP-3-HEALTH-EBOLA-WHO.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">c05d70b4-b1e0-11f1-bf01-f73169dd863e</guid><pubDate>Wed, 16 Sep 2026 15:29:37 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/0fff06cc-b1e2-11f1-93b2-e5727ccb1647/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-09-16T144444Z-226795097-RC2EGNAD8RRF-RTRMADP-3-HEALTH-EBOLA-WHO.jpg">© Gradel Muyisa Mumbere, Reuters</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>At least 82 killed in gold mine collapse in remote area of Sudan, paramilitary RSF say</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260916-sudan-gold-mine-collapse-kills-at-least-67-people</link><description>
A gold mine collapse on Wednesday killed at least 82 people in a remote area in Sudan, a source in the paramilitary Rapid Support Forces reported. The destruction occurred at the al-Zara gold mine, located in West Kordofan province – an area under the control of the RSF, who have been at war with the country’s military for over three years.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/70695fe0-b1c3-11f1-b6db-f72ea3a769d9/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26173615409174.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/70695fe0-b1c3-11f1-b6db-f72ea3a769d9/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26173615409174.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">773faf00-b1c2-11f1-a90f-af3f495acf71</guid><pubDate>Wed, 16 Sep 2026 11:54:54 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/70695fe0-b1c3-11f1-b6db-f72ea3a769d9/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26173615409174.jpg">© Mohnd Bla, AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Russian war, African recruits</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/russian-war-african-recruits</link><description>
In this edition: thousands of Africans have found themselves in Ukraine fighting or working on behalf of the Kremlin. We speak to a journalist who just got back from Ukraine. Also, in the Democratic Republic of Congo, the C64 opposition coalition held a demonstration in the capital Kinshasa. And finally, Kenya's celebrating a stellar athletics victory as the country beats London in the race to host the 2029 World Championships.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/5f5b5b64-b14f-11f1-bce0-53b7617a3a51/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-21125713136aa9bd7c3552a5-36969276.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/5f5b5b64-b14f-11f1-bce0-53b7617a3a51/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-21125713136aa9bd7c3552a5-36969276.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">8a8fb3d2-b14c-11f1-8289-05b4ed321f9c</guid><pubDate>Tue, 15 Sep 2026 22:13:20 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Georja CALVIN-SMITH</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Nigeria's Dangote refinery launches biggest African IPO</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260915-nigeria-s-dangote-refinery-launches-biggest-african-ipo-for-retail-investors</link><description>
Africa’s richest man Aliko Dangote on Monday launched an IPO for his $19 billion Nigerian oil refinery, seeking $1.6 billion from retail investors across the continent. The offering values Africa’s largest refinery at about $49 billion and has drawn both strong investor interest and concerns over its high valuation.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/17aad168-b05b-11f1-b58b-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/732cb7dc19c595839564de7f85f58c87ed0476c1.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/17aad168-b05b-11f1-b58b-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/732cb7dc19c595839564de7f85f58c87ed0476c1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">2b86e24c-b099-11f1-93af-6b224e4d46e2</guid><pubDate>Tue, 15 Sep 2026 00:05:32 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/17aad168-b05b-11f1-b58b-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/732cb7dc19c595839564de7f85f58c87ed0476c1.jpg">© Toyin Adedokun, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Nigerian oil refinery Dangote aims to raise 1.6 billion dollars</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/nigerian-oil-refinery-dangote-aims-to-raise-1-6-billion-dollars</link><description>
In tonight's edition: Nigeria's Dangote refinery opens itself to public ownership with plans to raise $1.6 billion dollars from retail investors across the continent. Also, DR Congo’s President Felix Tshisekedi calls on the National Service to clean up the streets of Kinshasa. And with a month and a half to go until the Youth Olympic Games begin in Senegal, the Olympic flame begins its tour of the host country.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/62c362ea-b07a-11f1-be3d-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/DN196787-A-01-20260908-01.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/62c362ea-b07a-11f1-be3d-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/DN196787-A-01-20260908-01.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">fded387c-b07a-11f1-843e-69c181e861bd</guid><pubDate>Mon, 14 Sep 2026 20:31:28 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Clémence WALLER</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Nigeria's Dangote refinery launches continent's biggest IPO</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260914-nigeria-s-dangote-refinery-launches-continent-s-biggest-ipo</link><description>
Nigerian billionaire Aliko Dangote on Monday launched what is slated to be Africa's biggest initial public offering (IPO), aiming to raise $1.6 billion in part to expand his mega oil refinery into the world's largest.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/578763b4-b056-11f1-965f-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/88a158ddf4716b6fb1931ddd52e7a887f109b68f.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/578763b4-b056-11f1-965f-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/88a158ddf4716b6fb1931ddd52e7a887f109b68f.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">c38add4c-b02a-11f1-ac74-033ed0845cfa</guid><pubDate>Mon, 14 Sep 2026 10:55:13 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/578763b4-b056-11f1-965f-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/88a158ddf4716b6fb1931ddd52e7a887f109b68f.jpg">© Temiloluwa Johnson, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Children die in a school fire in Bukavu</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/eye-on-africa/20260911-children-die-in-a-school-fire-in-bukavu</link><description>
In this edition: children have died after a school fire broke out in the M23 rebel occupied city of Bukavu, Democratic Republic of Congo. Also, Senegal looks to the skies as it seeks to develop its home-grown satellite industry. And finally, we wonder about the future of African cities. How will they cope with the population boom and climate crisis? Some at the Kenya architecture biennale in Nairobi tried to answer these questions.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/e54c4920-ae1d-11f1-ae7e-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-10961424326aa461fc83f3e6-90411594.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/e54c4920-ae1d-11f1-ae7e-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-10961424326aa461fc83f3e6-90411594.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">96972eb8-ae1c-11f1-88ff-eb2137a3fed2</guid><pubDate>Fri, 11 Sep 2026 20:18:17 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Clémence WALLER</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Ugandan royal succession scandal</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/ugandan-royal-succession-scandal</link><description>
In tonight's edition, a dramatic struggle for control of a throne in Uganda. Also, Rwanda shut down 101 alcoholic beverage factories last month and declared a public health crisis after at least 50 people were killed by contaminated illegal supplies. And Ethiopia celebrates its new year. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/13a0f8d6-ad54-11f1-a4bc-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/WB231224-O-01-20260910-01.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/13a0f8d6-ad54-11f1-a4bc-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/WB231224-O-01-20260910-01.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">cb5b032c-ad54-11f1-8b14-0f702f747911</guid><pubDate>Thu, 10 Sep 2026 20:20:01 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Georja CALVIN-SMITH</dc:creator></item><item><category>Tête à tête</category><title>'Infantino is the one who has brought the most to Africa' says Samuel Eto'o</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/t%C3%AAte-%C3%A0-t%C3%AAte/20260910-infantino-is-the-one-who-has-brought-the-most-to-africa-says-samuel-eto-o</link><description>
In an interview with FRANCE 24, former football star and current president of the Cameroonian Football Federation Samuel Eto'o spoke about the recent backlash towards FIFA president Gianni Infantino and confirmed he will support him for re-election because of "everything he has brought to African football", adding that the African federations will defend what seems "right to us given the challenges that we face in our own federations".
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/fcb94cba-acf7-11f1-b197-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/ETOO.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/fcb94cba-acf7-11f1-b197-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/ETOO.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">7c63acba-ad09-11f1-8646-47d8210ea19e</guid><pubDate>Thu, 10 Sep 2026 14:31:49 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Marc PERELMAN</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Algeria cuts diplomatic ties with United Arab Emirates over 'provocative and hostile actions'</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260910-algeria-cuts-diplomatic-ties-with-united-arab-emirates-over-provocative-and-hostile-actions</link><description>
Algeria is severing diplomatic ties with the United Arab Emirates, state media reported Thursday. Algerian President ‌Abdelmadjid Tebboune has in the past accused the Gulf state of trying to meddle in Algeria's internal affairs.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/bc05aaf6-ad0b-11f1-83d5-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/AP25106649315369.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/bc05aaf6-ad0b-11f1-83d5-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/AP25106649315369.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">5432f6e0-ad0b-11f1-b507-47d8210ea19e</guid><pubDate>Thu, 10 Sep 2026 11:50:14 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/bc05aaf6-ad0b-11f1-83d5-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/AP25106649315369.jpg">© Fateh Guidoum, AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Aid cuts force clinic closures across Sudan</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/aid-cuts-force-clinic-closures-across-sudan</link><description>
