</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a9eb4f4c-ac39-11f1-9e39-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-9172062096aa135946cc533-84303831.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a9eb4f4c-ac39-11f1-9e39-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-9172062096aa135946cc533-84303831.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">538ed126-abc3-11f1-af2d-5d25218809f1</guid><pubDate>Tue, 08 Sep 2026 20:29:13 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Georja CALVIN-SMITH</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Egyptian influencer faces death penalty over drug charges</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/egyptian-influencer-s-death-penalty-over-drug-charges</link><description>
In tonight's edition: An Egyptian TV presenter is among a dozen people a court has sentenced to death on drug charges. But first: a South African high court rejects a former defence minister's bid to have a corruption case against her dropped. Plus the very first Pan-African Architecture Biennale opened its doors on Monday in the Kenyan capital Nairobi.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/fa2b8154-ab5b-11f1-b0b6-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-16418034546a9fc1a6ceaea1-62503273.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/fa2b8154-ab5b-11f1-b0b6-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-16418034546a9fc1a6ceaea1-62503273.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">7924cd94-aaf8-11f1-9ce0-3bbf879cc2fc</guid><pubDate>Mon, 07 Sep 2026 20:27:19 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Georja CALVIN-SMITH</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Tunisians tell the story of their summer of shortages</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260907-tunisians-tell-story-their-summer-shortages</link><description>
Frequent water and power cuts, long queues for bottled water, poultry farms destroyed by the heat: these have been the scenes across Tunisia this summer as millions of people were affected by shortages in electricity and water. Our Observers say this is the result of years of underinvestment in infrastructure while the Tunisian president, for his part, blames "deliberate and planned acts of sabotage”. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/bd7419ca-a85d-11f1-8484-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/photo-de-Matthew-Metro-sur-son-profil-Facebook-27.png"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/bd7419ca-a85d-11f1-8484-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/photo-de-Matthew-Metro-sur-son-profil-Facebook-27.png" type="image/png" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">2b84e832-aadd-11f1-83c2-e38bbda28610</guid><pubDate>Mon, 07 Sep 2026 18:50:16 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/bd7419ca-a85d-11f1-8484-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/photo-de-Matthew-Metro-sur-son-profil-Facebook-27.png">© X / TikTok</source><dc:creator>The FRANCE 24 Observers</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Egyptian TV presenter Sarah Khalifa sentenced to death in drug case</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260906-egyptian-tv-presenter-sarah-khalifa-sentenced-to-death-in-drug-case</link><description>
Sarah Khalifa, a well-known Egyptian TV presenter, has been sentenced to death for her involvement in a drug ring, state media reported over the weekend. Eleven others were also convicted in the case and handed a death sentence. Khalifa is best known for her program “Mission Impossible”.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/bc9ddf20-a9f4-11f1-8ad4-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/Capture-d-%C3%A9cran-2026-09-06-144741.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/bc9ddf20-a9f4-11f1-8ad4-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/Capture-d-%C3%A9cran-2026-09-06-144741.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">3415f0da-a9f8-11f1-bf5c-9b447c8e7aa4</guid><pubDate>Sun, 06 Sep 2026 13:52:42 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/bc9ddf20-a9f4-11f1-8ad4-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/Capture-d-%C3%A9cran-2026-09-06-144741.jpg">© Sarah Khalifa, Instagram</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Uber withdraws from Nigeria and Uganda</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/uber-withdraws-from-nigeria</link><description>
In tonight's programme: Uber is pulling out of Nigeria and Uganda, leaving thousands of drivers scrambling for new sources of income. But first: a UN report says foreign fighters and support networks are fuelling Sudan's war and worsening violence against civilians. Finally, we discuss the African Diaspora International Film Festival with co-director Diarah N'daw-Spech and Laurence Cole, co-producer of the Nigerian film "Shadow of Greed".
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/65d6d146-a8a1-11f1-9963-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-13640605226a9b2da1479d08-40791610.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/65d6d146-a8a1-11f1-9963-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-13640605226a9b2da1479d08-40791610.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">5786c5e8-a8a0-11f1-8f6e-c70340fa1871</guid><pubDate>Fri, 04 Sep 2026 20:39:08 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Hélène Daouphars</dc:creator></item><item><category>Spotlight</category><title>'Moroccans are desperate: Years of political crackdown and lack of economic opportunities'</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20260904-moroccans-are-desperate-years-of-political-crackdown-and-lack-of-economic-opportunities</link><description>
François Picard is pleased to welcome Aida Alami, New York Times World contributor. Drawing on her own field reporting at the Moroccan border, Alami challenges attempts to explain the mass crossing to the Spanish enclave of Ceuta primarily through a theory of deliberate state orchestration. While acknowledging both Morocco's history of leveraging migration in its relations with Spain and the possibility that security forces made tactical decisions on the day, Alami insists on distinguishing evidence from inference.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c2563506-a85b-11f1-87ff-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-11800630236a9ab8cba12d07-46229679.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c2563506-a85b-11f1-87ff-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-11800630236a9ab8cba12d07-46229679.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">7006f03c-a85c-11f1-a672-6be82c255321</guid><pubDate>Fri, 04 Sep 2026 12:30:37 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>François PICARD</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Mass burial reveals hidden toll of Algeria’s deadly ‘Black Wednesday’ wildfires</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260904-mass-burial-algeria-black-wednesday-deadly-wildfires</link><description>
Wildfires ripped across northeastern Algeria in late August. Photos and videos showing the mass burial of several dozen people have raised questions about the real death toll of the fires as Algerian authorities haven’t shared new numbers since August 26. A number of Moroccan social media accounts have also been sharing disinformation about the fires.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a2361f4c-a6ea-11f1-9d5a-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/cover-129.png"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a2361f4c-a6ea-11f1-9d5a-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/cover-129.png" type="image/png" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">2112e918-a82f-11f1-890f-0bbf4a89853b</guid><pubDate>Fri, 04 Sep 2026 11:33:04 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a2361f4c-a6ea-11f1-9d5a-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/cover-129.png">© Observers</source><dc:creator>The FRANCE 24 Observers</dc:creator></item></channel></rss>
